WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:08.390
Hi, I'm Kirill.

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:14.020
And I want to tell a few
stories about the dm package

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:16.990
and show recent developments.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:21.460
In a nutshell, dm helps when
we have multiple tables in R.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:24.040
Some call this a
relational data model.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:27.390
So we had computers
for sale in the '50s.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:30.170
We started to think about
databases in the '60s.

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:35.680
In 1970, Edgar Codd published
a relational model,

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:40.410
and this is way before I was
born and still in use today.

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.360
An example.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.270
The points at the right
are energy consumers

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:50.510
in Switzerland, stored in rows
in the table at the left.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:53.880
And there is a lot of detail
in all those related tables.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.705
And this looks scary.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:00:59.520
With our client,

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.270
we wanted to build
a Shiny app that

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:06.180
allows filtering across
all those related tables.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.150
This shows the

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:11.020
Shiny app in action.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:14.970
All we had back then was a tool
to visualize the relationships.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:19.360
We did not have tools that
made the task less scary.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:21.380
So we went ahead.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:24.430
We implemented a routine
that did all those joins

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:27.770
and filters in
800 lines of code.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:29.950
It worked, but it was tedious.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.650
It was difficult to
maintain, it was error-prone,

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:36.690
and we thought we can
do better than that.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:38.910
And that's how dm was born.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:43.350
With the dm package,
the filtering logic

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:47.130
was reduced to a
variant of this.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:50.040
So from 800 down to six.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.680
And the best thing is, if
the data model changes,

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.340
the code stays the same.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:55.310
I call it a success.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.210
Others call it a success, too.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:04.420
It's amazing.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:05.300
It's a treasure.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:06.290
It's a lifesaver.

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.139
Thanks, Garrick.

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:09.789
Thanks, Hadley.

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:10.840
Thanks, Nils.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:15.340
Thanks to everybody who filed
issues, who contributed code

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:16.320
to the package.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.300
You are awesome.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:23.190
Best thing is we are
on CRAN in version 1.0.0.

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:25.580
R-universe and GitHub as well.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:27.420
So if you want the
latest and greatest,

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.020
you may want to go
to R-universe.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.680
If you don't want to mess
up your local installation,

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.650
click this Posit Cloud button.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:41.950
This gives you a... well,
should I say RStudio Cloud?

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:43.780
At some point, this will change.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.150
IDE in your browser
with everything

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:47.930
installed so you can just test.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:51.270
So there is no excuse
to not try it today.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:52.940
All right.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:56.000
Some people are wary
about dependencies.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.638
We are not among those people.

00:02:57.638 --> 00:02:59.180
So in fact dm
wouldn't have been

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:04.710
possible without all those
great packages we depend upon.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.430
Without R Core, without
tidyverse, without igraph.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:08.420
Thanks, Gabor.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.030
Without well, everything.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:16.030
Thanks. This is us.

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.010
And I always wanted
to bring this,

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:20.170
but I'm repurposing this today.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:24.330
So we're talking
about multiple tables.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:28.000
So which of these tools has a
concept of an active dataset?

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.030
Right, you start a
program, you open a file,

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:33.280
and this is your data set
and you work with it, right?

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:37.360
It is one car, one data set,
many cars, many data sets.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:40.380
In R, we don't have
the active data set.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.378
That's a good thing.

00:03:42.378 --> 00:03:43.920
It took me a while
to realize there's

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:48.360
a difference between data
for analysis and data

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:49.900
cleaning, data management.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.110
So for analysis,
all we really want

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:57.060
is tidy data: rows, columns,
cells with atomic values,

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:01.510
everything lined up cleanly
ready to put into our model.

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:03.660
Except there is
duplication in those

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.450
highlighted parts at
left, and we can't

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:07.720
have that for data management.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:10.180
We can't have that
for data cleaning.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.910
This leads to problems
further down the road.

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:17.220
So if we try a different
approach, wide form,

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:18.380
this is better.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:22.079
There is no duplication except
that those beautiful column

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:24.190
names,

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:27.220
make me want to turn
this into long form

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:28.870
the first time I see it.

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:36.360
So we want multiple
tables we need often.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.880
Multiple tables
and we want dm

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.770
because that's what
dm is doing.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:45.540
If you have more than one table,
dm makes your life easier.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.200
We are in the database session.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:51.820
So many of you will know about
primary keys and foreign keys.

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:54.240
This is just to make very sure
we're all on the same page

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.350
very briefly.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:58.080
The data set we saw in
the previous slides could

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.130
be decomposed into two tables.

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:03.690
Measurements at the left,
entities at the right.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:07.730
So multiple measurements
correspond to a single entity.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:09.990
The entities have a primary key.

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:13.770
The ID column, the measurements

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:16.590
have a foreign key
also in the ID column

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:19.260
linked to the entities.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:25.050
And in fact, this is what a
dm object contains: tables.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:29.730
This could be local data frames
or lazy tables on the database

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:32.440
with the dbplyr package.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:36.690
Maybe arrow also works, but
I'm not sure we need it.

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:38.020
More on that

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:41.580
on the last slide.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:45.390
We have primary keys ID and
we have foreign keys that

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:47.080
link tables together.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.183
That's a dm object, but.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:55.940
Do we really need a new package?

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:58.770
For 2 tables, for 5 tables.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:00.590
Even for 10 tables.

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:02.000
Just worked before.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.080
Will it actually make
your life easier?

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:10.080
Will it integrate into
your existing workflows?

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.240
Will it help you in your work?

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:15.550
And I believe the
answer is yes, yes, yes.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:17.640
And I'll show you how.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:22.930
So this is a very new thing,
a Shiny app we have built.

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:26.050
And the team at cynkra
helped a lot with that.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:29.160
So thanks here for
all the support

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:32.400
I got when preparing this.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.680
This is the AdventureWorks
data set

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.250
if you're familiar with that.

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:40.780
And that's the
Shiny connection.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:42.880
So if we start
from the database,

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:45.040
we can with pointing
and clicking,

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:48.440
select the tables we care about.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:51.880
We can create code.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:56.770
That we can run immediately,
we can store it in an R script,

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:58.670
we can put this into a function.

00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:02.320
So David here will talk
more about how to turn this

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:03.800
into a real R package.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:07.170
But for us, this kind of works.

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:10.290
So you run this Shiny app once.

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:13.980
This is pretty much
like the RStudio/Posit

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:19.030
IDE Import Assistant.

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:20.760
It will give you code
that you can reuse.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:25.060
This is just a summary.

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:29.820
So selecting tables
gives you code

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:34.350
which you can copy to
your console at once.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:37.577
For the data frames use case,
when you start with data frame,

00:07:37.577 --> 00:07:39.910
data frames don't know
about primary, foreign keys.

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:45.110
They have just rows and
columns, so we have to teach

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:48.530
dm what those keys are,
and we can do this as well

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:49.880
with the Shiny app.

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:53.690
So we're selecting
the tables, we're

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:55.370
selecting the columns
that constitute

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:57.800
the primary keys and
the foreign keys,

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:03.790
and we get an instant
preview in the diagram.

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:05.620
Same here we get the code.

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:08.440
Plus, we can also
experiment with colors.

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:11.980
I find this very helpful in
guiding the eye in the more

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:13.810
complex data model.

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:16.390
There is an undo
function if we mess up.

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:20.830
If you're satisfied,
the copy to console button

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:23.770
will give you the code
for this data model

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:26.940
from your data frames.

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:28.000
So that's new.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:32.070
That's in dm version 1.0.1.

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:34.309
And yeah.

00:08:34.309 --> 00:08:35.320
To sum up.

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:37.340
Works for

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:41.669
databases, for tables on the
database, works for data frames.

00:08:41.669 --> 00:08:42.290
Try it out.

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:48.460
In our consulting
work, we are using

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:50.483
dm, well, you saw the
expression whenever

00:08:50.483 --> 00:08:51.900
we have more than
one table, dm

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:55.210
is just the basic
thing that we do.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:59.990
We used it to help
with a Shiny app

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:02.930
that handles contracts.

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:07.060
So, this is a situation where we

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:11.170
have for

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:13.510
a contract partner.

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:17.660
First stage, contracts at the
level of individual countries.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:21.400
And then the second stage, we
would pool contracts together

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:26.540
to get an overarching
query for this customer.

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:31.040
And this shows the database
UI that we have contributed.

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:33.410
We have projects,
quote, family quotes.

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:36.280
And this maps to this
data model that we

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:39.370
have designed with
the dm package

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:42.980
and applied to the database
as soon as we are ready.

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:46.870
And we could also
continuously update it

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:49.870
because the data model
is specified in code.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:53.780
So the first stage is everything
that's orangish at the bottom.

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:57.050
And what we can do
with dm very easily

00:09:57.050 --> 00:10:00.320
for a specific quote which we
identify with the primary key,

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:04.550
the quote ID, we can
get all the related

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:09.080
data in one swoop
with the dm package.

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:11.140
Load it locally.

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:15.330
Update what we need based on
the input from the Shiny app.

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:16.780
Put it back to the database.

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:20.280
So this is create, read, update,
delete for you in Shiny.

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:23.855
Made easy with the dm package
on a complex data model.

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:29.910
For data analysis.

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:32.110
So this is the
noisy flight data.

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:35.280
We use it throughout the
documentation of the flights

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:36.600
of the dm package.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:38.550
If we aggregate
the flight table,

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:42.390
let's say we group by origin,
summarize, compute number

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:47.790
of flights per origin airport,
or let's say mean arrival delay.

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:48.815
What happens is

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:56.080
the result is a table that
is conceptually linked.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:57.820
With the airports table.

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:02.410
So this group by, summarize,
creates an implicit foreign key

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:06.340
into the airport table because
the data in the origin column

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:08.420
maps to what's been
in the airport.

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:10.450
And this link is
useful if we want

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:13.510
to later bring in
details from airports

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:16.905
or from other related
tables maybe not shown here.

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:23.200
How does the dm package
make it easy

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:26.730
to keep track of your keys?

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:29.620
We have a new feature.

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:32.990
We call it deconstruct,
reconstruct.

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:36.950
You can go from a dm
object, from a data model

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:39.590
to individual tables
that still know

00:11:39.590 --> 00:11:42.520
about their primary
and foreign keys.

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:44.660
I'm not going to
tell you how, but we

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:47.780
can talk about this later.

00:11:47.780 --> 00:11:49.880
We can apply data
transformations

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:52.550
on those separate
tables with dplyr, tidyr

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:55.220
or whatever works
for you, and we

00:11:55.220 --> 00:11:59.150
can put this back
into a relational data

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:00.490
model as a dm object.

00:12:03.790 --> 00:12:04.850
A bit of code.

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:08.970
So the new dm_deconstruct
function

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:10.840
There's nothing
except printing code.

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:15.000
So from a dm object that
has tables, primary keys

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:16.200
and foreign keys.

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:22.950
It will give you a code that
gets you those separate tables.

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:26.220
Which you then can compute on.

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:31.060
So keyed = TRUE is
kind of the key here.

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:33.820
The constructor on
those objects will

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:35.337
result in an object
that still has

00:12:35.337 --> 00:12:37.170
all those primary and
foreign keys in place.

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:46.290
And this also works when
we have analyzed the data.

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:50.110
So here the new table will

00:12:50.110 --> 00:12:53.250
contribute one primary
key, one foreign key.

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:55.720
I'm very excited
about this feature.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:59.170
We had a similar concept
called zooming before,

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:01.420
but this of course
doesn't integrate as well

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:03.100
with existing workflows.

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:06.750
So I think this is a
step towards helping

00:13:10.210 --> 00:13:13.960
adapt dm to
existing codes so

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:16.510
that you could
benefit from dm even

00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:18.390
if you're not starting
on the green field.

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:23.780
That's what the resulting
dm object looks like.

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:34.700
I've had this before.

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:35.780
Why one data model?

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:36.780
Why just one data model?

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:37.820
We can do more.

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:42.020
So this is an example
from another project

00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:44.230
with another customer.

00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:46.710
The notes,

00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:49.490
the boxes in this diagrams
are whole data models.

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:52.190
The arrows represent
data transformations.

00:13:52.190 --> 00:13:55.140
This is an ETL process.

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:58.320
Extract, transform, load.

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:04.500
Targeting whole
data models, data model objects,

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:07.710
whole data set going from
data set to data set,

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:10.980
giving better guarantees,
moving to the right

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:12.130
in terms of data quality.

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:14.150
Data integrity.

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:16.470
And so forth.

00:14:16.470 --> 00:14:19.020
We don't have a specific
framework to do this.

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:21.120
This is right now all custom.

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:24.990
This could be a blog post,
but we have a pretty good idea

00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:28.020
how to do this because there
is an inherent chicken and egg

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:29.280
problem here.

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:33.240
When populating a
database, we first

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:34.930
need to create a structure.

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:40.580
The tables with the columns
and the relationships

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:43.600
in an empty form before
we can fill them.

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:45.700
And how do we know
this structure?

00:14:45.700 --> 00:14:49.440
So the dm_ptype function
helps us, which

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:54.380
gives us the prototype of

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:55.650
the dm object.

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:57.500
Meaning?

00:14:57.500 --> 00:14:59.810
tables and relationships
minus the data,

00:14:59.810 --> 00:15:05.810
which we then can write to
the database and populate.

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:10.190
I want to spend the last
few minutes on a new idea

00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:13.100
that didn't make
it into dm 1.0.0

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:16.670
but that we are
also excited about.

00:15:16.670 --> 00:15:18.980
Normalized data has
been around for ages

00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:21.510
and will stay around. At the
other end of the spectrum.

00:15:21.510 --> 00:15:24.470
We have deeply nested data,
so when you query a web API,

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:27.530
typically what you get
is JSON deeply nested

00:15:27.530 --> 00:15:32.580
and somewhere in the middle
we have nested tables.

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:35.610
With the tidyr package
think of nest and unnest, pack and unpack.

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:38.930
Arrow has great
support for this in.

00:15:41.590 --> 00:15:43.360
In their data format.

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:45.280
And what we can
do in the dm today

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:51.510
is get from normalized data from
a dm object to nested tables.

00:15:51.510 --> 00:15:52.390
To a single nested

00:15:52.390 --> 00:15:56.030
table and back.

00:15:56.030 --> 00:15:57.030
More or less losslessly,

00:15:57.030 --> 00:15:58.530
more or
less in all cases.

00:15:58.530 --> 00:15:59.900
We're working on that.

00:15:59.900 --> 00:16:03.440
But this idea looks
very appealing,

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:06.770
in particular with
the very nice

00:16:06.770 --> 00:16:10.060
tibblify package, which I
think is really underrated.

00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:14.060
We can go from deeply nested

00:16:14.060 --> 00:16:17.820
to nested tibbles
in a controlled way,

00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:20.090
and the way back to
JSON is understood.

00:16:20.090 --> 00:16:23.800
So with all those bits and
pieces in place, I hope,

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:29.830
very soon to be able to present
a way to seamlessly move

00:16:29.830 --> 00:16:31.250
between all those formats.

00:16:31.250 --> 00:16:36.130
So so this all of this becomes
one, and perhaps nested tables

00:16:36.130 --> 00:16:38.440
are our middle ground because
this would allow us

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:41.770
to go back to one single table.

00:16:41.770 --> 00:16:45.620
That's just easier than
all those boxes and arrows.

00:16:45.620 --> 00:16:46.420
We are cynkra.

00:16:49.130 --> 00:16:51.120
The team, as I
said, helped a lot.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:56.870
Preparing the Shiny app,
making sure this all works.

00:16:56.870 --> 00:16:57.830
Thanks here.

00:16:57.830 --> 00:17:00.210
We're very excited
to be a sponsor here.

00:17:00.210 --> 00:17:05.060
If you have detailed questions
or would like to chat

00:17:05.060 --> 00:17:08.480
or would like to have one
of those cheat sheets here.

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:10.460
They're also new, come by.

00:17:10.460 --> 00:17:13.400
Happy to talk or happy
to take questions here.

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:15.310
Thank you.

